Patch 7936 (fix, online): np_psnr learns a peer's remapped P2P port from the user id in its punch, uses the relay only when GET_CAPS says it carries this player's datagrams, and logs per-peer counters once per room.

test13 field (2-player "communication error", 2026-11-27): the joiner's router showed the game's P2P socket (UDP 3658) to the
server on public port 1369. np_psnr credited a punch or a datagram only from the peer's announced ip:port (peer_at), and the punch's
user id was ignored, so the remapped peer was never "heard" and its direct packets reached the game from an address it did not know.
After 3 s both sides moved the game's datagrams to the relay because HELLO_ACK's flag said the server relays, but a server under
`-require-pass relay` drops every relayed datagram of a player without a relay pass (re-psn relay.go, relay_no_pass); its docs say
only GET_CAPS bit 5 tells whether this player can use the relay. The game gave up its join after 15 s.
libs/network/np_psnr.c:
- A punch ("PSNR" 02 | user u32) is matched by its user id first. From the announced address it marks the peer heard, as before.
  From the same public IP on another port, that endpoint is learned (seen_ip/seen_port): the peer counts as heard, two punches go
  back to it so its side hears us, the title's datagrams to the announced address are sent there directly (np_psnr_p2p_route
  sends them and returns 1), and datagrams from it reach the title rewritten to the announced address. A punch naming a known user
  from another IP teaches nothing (a stranger who guesses a user id can't move a peer's traffic). A punch whose user is unknown
  falls back to the address match. PSNR_PUNCH_LEARN=0: nothing is learned (the old behaviour); such punches are still counted.
- After HELLO, np_psnr_connect sends GET_CAPS (np_psnr_request, answered on the pump). The relay is used for datagrams (relay_udp)
  and streams (np_psnr_stream_connect; otherwise -2, direct) only when CAPS has PSNR_CAP_RELAY. A server older than GET_CAPS
  (ERROR bad request) has no passes, so its HELLO_ACK flag stands; any other answer, or none yet: no relay. One log line says which,
  and a peer still unheard after 3 s with no relay gets one line saying its datagrams stay direct. PSNR_RELAY_NEEDS_CAPS=0: the
  HELLO_ACK flag alone, as before.
- Per-peer counters for the room: the title's datagrams heard directly, relayed out and in, punches from the right user on another
  port (and the last such port). np_psnr_peer_stats(user) / _locked print one line per peer (user id and ports only, no address)
  and restart them.
- A relayed datagram naming user 0 no longer matches an empty peer slot (it was handed to the title from 0.0.0.0:0).
libs/network/sceNpMatching2.c: MEMBER_LEFT logs that member's counters, KICKED and the LeaveRoom reply log every peer's. MEMBER_LEFT
and KICKED now take the np_psnr peer lock before the state lock, as MEMBER_JOINED does; the LeaveRoom reply logs after the state lock
is released.
Tests: libs/network/tests/test_np_nat.c (remapped port learned from the punch, not from another IP, the route and the rewrite, the
switch off; the relay decision for CAPS with and without bit 5, a pre-GET_CAPS server, an error, no answer, the switch; the counters),
built and run on Windows by installer/tools/np_nat_test.sh. test_np_matching2.c gets the two new stubs. Not run against a real
server or router; matching2_context_test.py (posix only) not run.

